hello and welcome this is daniel  and welcome to another video made  exclusively for youtube now  in this video i'm very excited um to be  starting a new series that i hinted  hinted at in previous videos and in this  series  i will be examining common mistakes the  most common mistakes  that chess players in the beginner and  intermediate levels  tend to make and i haven't decided how  many of these videos  i'll produce but probably it'll be on  the order of 10  and what i'll do is each video will be  dedicated to one particular mistake that  is very very common at the beginner and  club player levels  and in order to illustrate this mistake  i'll draw on my own tournament  experience  i'll show how i was frequently the one  committing these mistakes  uh what i learned from these games in  which i committed them  and how we can learn from that  experience  um in order to try to minimize those  mistakes so  without further ado um  i present to you the first mistake that  i'd like to talk about  which in short is forgetting  that tactics in the end game exist  now let's unpack that statement for a  second what does that mean well  i'm not going to delve too deeply into  defining what an end game is  you probably have a good understanding  of what that is it's a position  generally without queens  and generally with a limited amount of  material on the board  and when there is a limited amount of  material on the board what  generally tends to happen is that you  know because of the limited resources  players assume that  you can kind of switch off your tactical  detector there's going to be no danger  to the kings  there's going to be no abundance of  tactical threats no combinations no  sacrifices and  all of that stems from the fact that  well other than the king there's simply  not that many pieces left on the board  and it's crucial to remember that end  games  are hot beds of tactics and combinations  and even though there might not be a lot  of pieces left on the board  tactics are still extremely prevalent  and you have to keep your tactical  detector switched on  even when you think that you're safe and  the position in front of you is a great  illustration that it's a game that i  will never forget  now this position i had the black pieces  uh against  an international master from france  anthony balesh  and we're looking at it from anthony's  perspective  this game was played in philadelphia  2009. as you can see i was  rated approximately 2300 so you know i  was already pretty high rated  and i had played a very very good game  and reached an end game that in my mind  was very easily drawn  now what immediately should jump out at  you is the fact that white is up a pawn  however it's a pretty lame pawn if i may  say so myself it's this age pawn  and there's very very few pieces left on  the board now  if you have a nodding acquaintance with  theoretical end games that you will know  that simply having an h pawn and kings  is a dead draw  so trades here are in black's favor and  i figured that if i could just rid  myself of the immediate threats  then i should have no problem securing a  draw and of course i was right  now the next question is well what are  these immediate threats  let's imagine that it's white to move in  this position pause the video for a  second try to figure out what it is that  white's going to do here  well it's not too hard to realize that  white has a very very serious threat  he's threatening to play rook h4 check  king has to move to g8  and now white has checkmate knight e7  checkmate so you can see that  checkmate's do a rise in the end game  and  notice how the king on g6 is  facilitating this whole enterprise  without the king  black's king would have would have had  an escape square and f7  now of course i saw this i didn't miss  this because i also knew my opponent had  played rook before  i smelled a rat and so i figured he was  threatening checkmate  now i thought okay all that i really  need to do  is cut into this mechanism somehow now i  figured well i can't really  defend the square on h4 so what i can do  i thought is i can protect the e7 square  and so i played the very sensible  looking move rook to e8  and my idea was that after rook h4 check  king g8  my opponent would no longer be able to  give me a check on e7 with the knight  that would simply give up the knight  and all other attempts that my opponent  has in this position fall by the wayside  if he goes knight f6 check well it looks  like a fork but  the reality is i just take then i swing  my rook back now in  future videos i'll talk about these  types of end games for now suffice it to  say that  black is a very very easy draw because  white simply cannot make progress  black's going to give a bunch of checks  on the  back of the board to the white king and  once again rook trades are in blacks  the rook trade is in black's favor so i  would i would have had no problem  securing the draw here  so i and if white doesn't go if if after  a rook to e8  in this position y doesn't go knight f6  and let's say white  um swings his rook to g4 for example  well white is actually liable to get  even a worse position because i'm going  to give him a check on e6 i'm going to  push the king back from g6  and white actually has to be careful  here to make a draw because if he plays  king f5  once again never switch off your  tactical detector rookie 5 check is a  fork  king has to move back rook takes d5 and  now white has a very arduous task  of making a draw in this position which  by the way is possible but not easy  and if in this position after rookie 6  jack white  goes kh5 well among other things black  can simply go rook h6 check  skewering the king and picking off the  pawn  and draw agreement is on the cards  now in playing rookie 8 i had only  checked  that i was stopping the threat of rook  h497 and i had also made sure that the  move king f7  simply ran into rook cafe checking  essentially why it has to go back  but what i didn't do was explicitly  check for other  tactics i had only thought of it  in the sense of defending the e7 square  i didn't stop for a second say wait a  minute  let me look for some other tactical  possibilities in this position  and it turns out that white has an  absolutely stunning tactical idea  that turns the tables and actually wins  the game for white  once again i encourage you here to pause  the video take your time  and try to come up with this dastardly  move that my opponent sprung on me in  this position  now hopefully you were able to switch on  that tactical detector of yours  and realize that black's king is  actually still very much in grave danger  now the key  insight that i failed to make is that  the rook the black rook is  tied to the eighth rank and what does  that mean it's tied to the eighth rank  well that means  if the rook moves anywhere along this e  file let's imagine that the rook  ends up on e1 well then white has  backrank checkmate  uh black has apologies for this not sure  what this is  whoa okay my computer is  acting up there we go i think we're good  now uh white has  rook v8 check and it's background  checkmate i can make the very effective  defensive move rookie eight followed by  bishop  black is very close to drawing but  unfortunately ruby takes f8 wins the  game  and that might not seem like it's  important but  in fact it's extremely important why is  it extremely important because  white can use the fact that the rook is  tied down to play the brilliant move  knight e7 and when this move was played  i realized immediately  what was going on now what is going on  well white has posed the familiar threat  of checkmating on the side of the board  with rook h4  okay and black simply has no way of  stopping that  without giving up his bishop for example  which is of course disastrous  in addition white is still going to  checkmate black on h4 that doesn't even  help  and if i take on e7 and of course this  is what i missed and white has rook bhak  with checkmate to follow and if you look  at this position closely it's hard to  believe  but there's nothing that black can do  black cannot muster up sufficient  resources  to stop both threats at the same time if  he tries bishop h6  well that doesn't help because white  takes on h6 and if i take the knight now  nothing's changed this is still  checkmate after rookie rook takes e8  so amazingly the move 97 wins the game  for white  and i simply after staring at the  position for a couple of minutes  resigned  now if we go back to this position with  that understanding  we can now let me just remove these  arrows to make it  easier to visualize to make it easier to  look at this position  this arrow doesn't want this arrow  doesn't want to leave um  let me try to make it red first  make it green first ah it's okay  whatever i'll just leave this there oh  that's because it's white's most recent  move that's why  chess base has this thing where it just  shows you the most recent move in the  narrow  um what could black have done here well  had i realized that the king needs a lot  of space  there's a lot of moves here that draw i  could play the move rook to a8  and the idea is that 97 now meets with  rook a6 check pushing the king out of g6  um black could even play the move rook  to f1  and the idea is that rook b8 check meets  with rook f8 white hasn't gained  anything and if white goes  h4 check king g897 this is no longer  checkmate  as it used to be because black has  vacated this f8 square for his king  if white goes knight e7 here trying to  set up the same threats well again very  clearly this is not the same thing  because black and push off white's king  with rook g1 check  and then black is going to have the h7  square for his king so there were a lot  of moves here that  that drew i could have moved the rook on  the eighth file on the eighth rank i  could have moved the rook on the f file  all of that would have drawn anything  except the move that i played  so if you think about it i think that  the root cause of this mistake wasn't  just a random blunder it was the fact  that i wasn't checking for  mating tactics in this end game other  than the immediate threat of rook h4  because i assumed  that due to the limited resources my  opponent had there wouldn't be any such  combination  even possible and that is a big mistake  that i see made in a lot of games by a  lot of players  as soon as a lot of pieces come off the  board they stop looking for checkmates  they stop looking for combinations they  stop looking for sacrifices  they stop looking for attacks on the  king all of those things  are very much prevalent in end games in  fact a single king and pawn  can already mount an attack on the enemy  king that may sound absurd  but hopefully this example has convinced  you otherwise i remember that evening i  was  incredibly upset after this game but i  never forgot that lesson from that game  on  and although i still blunder a lot in  the end game  it rare is the occasion now that i  forget to check  for tactics and for combinations even  with a very limited amount of pieces on  the board  so this was our sort of first common  mistake that we've discussed which is  for getting to check for tactics in the  end game  i hope that you've enjoyed this video in  this example  in future videos we'll be talking about  other mistakes we'll be looking at other  examples this of course  is still a little bit experimental for  me but i hope that everybody was able to  take something out of it  and even if not then perhaps you just  enjoyed this nice little combination  i think 97 really is a beautiful move um  in retrospect of course i didn't think  so during the game  so uh in any case i hope you enjoyed i  would very much look forward to hearing  from you in the comments below any kind  of constructive criticism is very much  welcomed as this  is a new series and  uh above all whatever you do remember do  not  forget to check for tactics in the end  game  thank you

